# Copy to .env and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored (*.env in .gitignore).
# This file configures the server itself, which always runs on the host via
# `go run .`. docker-compose.local.yml runs the database only and reads nothing
# from here, so a stale value cannot repoint the container.

# ─── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CockroachDB Cloud, or any Postgres with the pgvector extension.
#
# Against the local stack, the container ports are published — use localhost,
# with the port of whichever database you started:
#   CockroachDB: postgresql://root@localhost:26257/defaultdb?sslmode=disable
#   PostgreSQL:  postgres://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/agent_space?sslmode=disable
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:26257/defaultdb?sslmode=verify-full

# Queue-driven investigations read the immutable, already-redacted context from
# static-log-analysis through a different connection. This user must have only
# CONNECT/USAGE and SELECT on investigations, incident_families, and
# investigation_contexts. Never point this at the writable agent database.
ANALYSIS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://analysis_context_reader:password@host:26257/static_log_analysis?sslmode=verify-full

# ─── LLM (any OpenAI-compatible endpoint) ────────────────────────────────────
# One variable names each model, wherever it runs. OPENROUTER_MODEL is sent to
# a local server unchanged, so switching endpoints does not mean switching
# settings.

OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
OPENROUTER_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.2
# OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen/qwen3-embedding-8b
# OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1024

# ── Run chat against your own server ──
# OPENAI_BASE_URL moves CHAT ONLY. Embeddings stay on OpenRouter, because the
# vector column is sized to the embedder at CREATE TABLE: moving chat to a local
# model must not silently swap a 1024-wide embedder for a 768-wide one and
# invalidate every stored vector. Chat is the expensive half worth running
# locally; embeddings are ~$0.01 per million tokens.
#
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1          # Ollama
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1           # vLLM
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080/v1           # llama.cpp, LM Studio
#
# From inside a container, a server on your host is host.docker.internal:
#   OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1
#
# Set OPENROUTER_MODEL to whatever name your server answers to. Leave it empty
# and "local-model" is sent: servers that serve one loaded model ignore the
# field, but ones that route by name (Ollama, vLLM) will reject it.
#
# The key is optional here — most local servers ignore it. OPENAI_API_KEY or
# LLM_API_KEY are used if set; OPENROUTER_API_KEY deliberately is not, so a live
# billable credential is never forwarded to an arbitrary process on localhost.
#   OPENAI_API_KEY=whatever-your-server-wants

# ── Run embeddings somewhere else too (fully offline) ──
# Only then does the request-width handling change: the OpenAI `dimensions`
# field is omitted, because most self-hosted embedding servers reject it, and
# the column width has to be stated instead (nomic-embed-text is 768,
# mxbai-embed-large 1024).
#   EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
#   OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
#   VECTOR_DIMENSIONS=768
#
# If your server does accept the field, opt back in and it sizes the column too:
#   OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=768
#
# Changing the width means recreating the tables:
#   go run ./cmd/nuke -mode=drop
#
# That drops the index, not the record. Engineers' decisions live in their own
# `decisions` table with the prose they were embedded from, so changing the
# embedding model costs a re-index rather than everything the team has decided.
# The incidents seeded through /store are not so lucky — reseed them.

# ─── CockroachDB MCP (the second CockroachDB integration) ────────────────────
# Cloud: create a service account API key in the Cloud Console under Access
# Management. COCKROACH_CLUSTER_ID is optional and scopes the agent to one
# cluster; omit it and the key's full RBAC scope applies.
COCKROACH_API_KEY=
COCKROACH_CLUSTER_ID=
# COCKROACH_MCP_URL=https://cockroachlabs.cloud/mcp

# Local: docker-compose.local.yml runs a self-hosted MCP server alongside
# CockroachDB. Point the server at it with:
#   COCKROACH_MCP_URL=http://localhost:8443
#   COCKROACH_API_KEY=local-dev-token-insecure   # = LOCAL_MCP_TOKEN below
#   COCKROACH_CLUSTER_ID=          # must be empty: scoping is a Cloud concept
#
# Leave COCKROACH_API_KEY empty and /tools and /agent report 503; the rest of
# the API works. That is also what happens on the pgvector-only path, since the
# MCP server reads crdb_internal and cannot talk to PostgreSQL.
#
# Overrides the local MCP server's bearer token. Minimum 16 characters — the
# server exits at boot if it is shorter.
# LOCAL_MCP_TOKEN=local-dev-token-insecure

# The agent is offered only the read-only MCP tools, so a wrong tool call cannot
# drop a table. Set this to hand it the write tools as well. Leave it unset.
# AGENT_ALLOW_WRITE_TOOLS=false

# Read-only tools the agent is nonetheless not offered, comma-separated.
# Defaults to show_statement,show_running_queries: cluster introspection that
# the prompt already discourages, and that does not return inside the client's
# 90s ceiling on a Cloud Basic cluster. Set it empty to offer everything.
# AGENT_EXCLUDE_TOOLS=show_statement,show_running_queries

# ─── SQS worker (assignments from static-log-analysis) ───────────────────────
# A background goroutine polls this queue and runs one investigation at a time,
# so an upstream detector can hand work over without holding an HTTP connection
# open for a 66s run. Collect the verdict with GET /agent/{investigation_id}.
#
# SQS_QUEUE_URL is the whole switch: leave it empty and the worker never starts,
# the HTTP API is unchanged, and nothing here matters. The agent is also needed
# — no MCP means no worker, for the same reason /agent returns 503.
#
# Locally, docker-compose.local.yml runs LocalStack and creates the queue:
#   SQS_QUEUE_URL=http://localhost:4566/000000000000/static-log-analysis
#   AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:4566
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=test
#   AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=test
#
# Against real SQS, point SQS_QUEUE_URL at the queue, set AWS_REGION to the
# region it actually lives in, and leave credentials to the standard chain
# (instance role, SSO, ~/.aws/credentials).
#
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL does not need deleting: a queue URL on amazonaws.com is not
# an address LocalStack can serve, so a leftover override is ignored and said so
# at boot. Without that, a half-finished switch sends every call to an emulator
# that is not listening, which reads as a broken queue rather than a stale .env.
#
# Keep WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS at or below the queue's own maxReceiveCount, so
# giving up on a message is something the API can report rather than a silent
# dead-letter.
SQS_QUEUE_URL=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# Trusted worker scope. These must exactly match the static-analysis deployment;
# message-body claims cannot override them.
AGENT_TENANT_ID=
AGENT_CLASSIFICATION=SENSITIVE
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL=

# How long a message stays invisible while being investigated. The worker
# renews this every third of the interval, so a run that outlives it is not
# handed to a second consumer — but the initial value still has to cover the
# gap before the first renewal.
# WORKER_VISIBILITY_TIMEOUT=120s

# Long-poll duration. SQS caps this at 20s; anything higher is clamped.
# WORKER_WAIT_TIME=20s

# Ceiling on one investigation, after which the run is abandoned and recorded
# as failed. Generous on purpose: the point of the queue path is that a slow
# provider day costs time, not the result.
#
# It also decides how long a row left at "running" is given the benefit of the
# doubt. A process that exits mid-run cannot write its own verdict, so at boot
# anything still running past this ceiling (plus five minutes) is marked failed.
# The work itself is not lost: SQS redelivers it and it is investigated again.
# WORKER_RUN_TIMEOUT=10m

# Delivery count at which a message is given up on, recorded as failed and
# deleted. Keep it at or below the queue's own maxReceiveCount so the failure
# is one the API can report, rather than a message that silently dead-letters.
# WORKER_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5

# ─── Remediation (turning a verdict into candidate fixes) ────────────────────
# Once an investigation decides HOTFIX and names a commit, the worker hands it
# to a pool that triages the fault, asks the model for several fixes, and runs
# each one in a throwaway container to find out whether it builds and passes.
# Read the result with GET /agent/{investigation_id}/remediation.
#
# This needs a container runtime. Without one the service logs why at boot and
# carries on investigating; nothing else changes. Prove the container half works
# before relying on it:
#   go run ./cmd/sandboxcheck                     # clone, build, test, diff
#   go run ./cmd/sandboxcheck -mode triage        # just the read-only gate
#
# ROLLBACK verdicts are never remediated: rolling back is not a code change.
#
# Set to false to turn it off on a machine that could otherwise run it. Unset
# means on.
# REMEDIATION_ENABLED=true

# The container CLI. podman and nerdctl work unchanged; only the name differs.
# CONTAINER_BINARY=docker

# How many investigations are remediated at once, and how many candidate
# containers each one fans out to. Two jobs of three candidates is six
# containers, which is about what a laptop will take.
# REMEDIATION_JOBS=2
# REMEDIATION_CANDIDATE_CONCURRENCY=3

# When a candidate applies but the build or the tests reject it, the failing
# output goes back to the model with its own file contents and the container
# runs again. The failures worth repairing are mechanical — a missing type
# conversion, an unused import — and one look is usually enough, while every
# round costs a container per candidate. Set to 0 to turn it off.
# REMEDIATION_MAX_REPAIRS=1

# Fine-grained PAT, used for two unrelated things: opening the draft PR when an
# engineer picks a candidate, and cloning inside the sandbox. The demo
# repository is public, so cloning needs no credential and none is passed into
# the container. Without this, fixes are still proposed and verified — picking
# one just cannot open a draft.
#
# It needs BOTH of these under Repository permissions, at
# https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens:
#     Contents:      Read and write
#     Pull requests: Read and write
#
# Selecting the repository is not enough on its own. A token with the repo in
# scope but no Contents grant reads fine and fails only on the first write, with
# "Resource not accessible by personal access token" — and the repository's own
# permissions block still reports push: true, because that is the account's
# access rather than the token's. Check which you have before relying on it:
#
#     ./scripts/tokencheck.sh
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ─── Frontend access ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Browser origins allowed to call this API. A Vite dev server on :5173 is a
# different origin from an API on :8080, so without this every request from it
# fails before it is sent.
#
# Unset means any origin, which is right for an unauthenticated service on a
# laptop and wrong the moment either of those changes. Set it alongside
# API_TOKEN below.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# ─── API access ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret for every route except /ping and /tools, which stay open.
# REQUIRED before exposing this server publicly: one /agent run is on the order
# of 15k tokens against a billable key, /retrieve reads stored incidents back
# out, and POST /solutions/{id}/pr opens a pull request on a real repository.
# Unset means no authentication at all, which the server warns about at boot.
#
# Clients send it as `X-Agent-Token: <token>` (or `Authorization: Bearer`).
# scripts/demo.sh and scripts/bench.sh pick it up from API_TOKEN automatically.
# API_TOKEN=
